---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'page', label: 'The practice page' },
   { id: 'axe', label: 'Run the audit' },
   { id: 'rules', label: 'Rules axe skips' },
   { id: 'tree', label: 'The accessibility tree' },
   { id: 'keyboard', label: 'Tab order' },
   { id: 'dialog', label: 'Opening the dialog' },
   { id: 'status', label: 'The confirmation message' },
   { id: 'passes', label: 'What axe called a pass' },
   { id: 'mcp', label: 'The same run from an agent' },
   { id: 'fixes', label: 'The ten fixes' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Practice on a broken page"
   summary="The docs site serves two copies of one team settings page. /demo/team has ten accessibility problems in it. /demo/team/fixed is the same page with all ten repaired. Run each command below against both URLs and compare the output. Three problems come back from axe-core, the open-source rule engine a1 axe runs inside the page. Two more come back only when you name the rule. The last five need the accessibility tree or a screen reader."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="panel" id="page">
      <h2>The practice page</h2>
      <p>
         Start the docs site, which serves both copies on port 4321.
      </p>
      <pre><code>npm run dev:docs</code></pre>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>/demo/team</code>
            <span>The page with the problems. Open <a href="/demo/team">it in a browser</a>.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>/demo/team/fixed</code>
            <span>The repaired page. Open <a href="/demo/team/fixed">it in a browser</a>.</span>
         </div>
      </div>
      <p>
         The page is a team settings screen. A table lists three members, each row holding an
         avatar, a role, a status pill, a last-active date, and an icon-only button that removes
         the member. Above the table sit a filter field, three status filter chips, and an
         <strong>Invite member</strong> button that opens a dialog with an email field, a role
         select, and a submit button. Submitting shows a confirmation above the table.
      </p>
      <p>
         Both URLs render <code>packages/docs/src/components/demo/team-settings.astro</code>,
         which takes one <code>fixed</code> prop. Every difference between the two pages is one of
         the ten fixes, so reading that file tells you exactly what changed.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="axe">
      <h2>Run the audit</h2>
      <p>
         <code>a1 audit</code> runs axe, reads the accessibility tree, and works out which WCAG
         success criteria the page triggers. A success criterion is one testable requirement in
         the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines, the standard most accessibility requirements
         come from. Start here on any page you have not checked before.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 audit http://localhost:4321/demo/team</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>audit  http://localhost:4321/demo/team

✗ 10 problems to fix.

Problems
  1.1.1  Non-text Content  [A]
      Images must have alternative text  critical
      3 failing elements:
        tr[data-status="active"][data-member-row=""]:nth-child(1) > td:nth-child(1) > .member > img
        ...
      Fix it:  a1 wcag rule image-alt
  4.1.2  Name, Role, Value  [A]
      Buttons must have discernible text  critical
      3 failing elements:
        tr[data-status="active"][data-member-row=""]:nth-child(1) > td:nth-child(5) > .icon-button[type="button"]
        ...
      Fix it:  a1 wcag rule button-name
  1.4.3  Contrast (Minimum)  [AA]
      Elements must meet minimum color contrast ratio thresholds  serious
      4 failing elements:
        tr[data-status="active"][data-member-row=""]:nth-child(1) > .faint
        tr[data-status="active"][data-member-row=""]:nth-child(2) > .faint
        .pill--pending
        and 1 more element
      Fix it:  a1 wcag rule color-contrast</code></pre>
      <p>
         Three rules, ten failing elements: the three avatar images have no <code>alt</code>
         attribute, each of the three remove buttons contains one <code>svg</code> and no text, and the
         Pending pill plus the three last-active dates are set in <code>#a2aab5</code>, which
         reaches 2.34:1 on white and 2.16:1 on the pill's <code>#f4f6f8</code> background where
         4.5:1 is the requirement.
      </p>
      <p>
         The bottom of the same report counts the page: <code>2 headings (level 1, 3)</code>.
         That is the fourth problem, and no rule in the default run reported it. The next section
         gets axe to.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 audit http://localhost:4321/demo/team/fixed</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>audit  http://localhost:4321/demo/team/fixed

✓ Nothing failed the automated checks.</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>a1 axe</code> alone gives the same three violations without the criterion rollup.
         Either command exits 4 on <code>/demo/team</code> and 0 on <code>/demo/team/fixed</code>,
         which is what makes them usable in a script. See
         <a href="/guides/scripting">script the CLI</a>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="rules">
      <div class="stack">
         <h2>Rules axe skips by default</h2>
         <p>
            A default run covers only the axe rules that map to a WCAG criterion. Two of the
            problems on this page belong to rules axe tags as best practice instead, so you have
            to name them with <code>--rule</code>.
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 axe http://localhost:4321/demo/team --rule heading-order</code></pre>
         <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>  ✗ heading-order  moderate
      Heading levels should only increase by one
      1 failing element
        .toolbar-heading
          &lt;h3 class="toolbar-heading"&gt;Members&lt;/h3&gt;</code></pre>
         <p>
            The second one needs the dialog open. axe reads the rendered page, and the dialog
            markup is hidden until the Invite member button runs. The demo page opens it for you
            when the URL ends in <code>#invite</code>.
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 axe 'http://localhost:4321/demo/team#invite' --rule aria-dialog-name</code></pre>
         <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>  ✗ aria-dialog-name  serious
      ARIA dialog and alertdialog nodes should have an accessible name
      1 failing element
        .modal
          &lt;div class="modal" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" data-invite-dialog=""&gt;
          Fix any of the following:
          • aria-label attribute does not exist or is empty
          • aria-labelledby attribute does not exist, references elements that do not exist or references elements that are empty
          • Element has no title attribute</code></pre>
         <p>
            The fixed page uses a native <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> with
            <code>aria-labelledby="invite-title"</code>. <code>aria-dialog-name</code> only
            checks elements carrying <code>role="dialog"</code>, so on the fixed page it reports
            neither a violation nor a pass. The next section confirms the name another way.
         </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">Scan what is rendered</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            Anything behind a click is invisible to <code>a1 axe</code>. Give the page a URL that
            opens the dialog, as this one does with <code>#invite</code>, or open it in a screen
            reader session and scan from there.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="tree">
      <h2>The accessibility tree</h2>
      <p>
         <code>a1 tree</code> prints the role and accessible name of every node a screen reader can
         reach, taken from Playwright's <code>ariaSnapshot()</code>. Run it against both dialogs.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 tree 'http://localhost:4321/demo/team#invite'
a1 tree 'http://localhost:4321/demo/team/fixed#invite'</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>- dialog:
  - heading "Invite member" [level=2]

- dialog "Invite member":
  - heading "Invite member" [level=2]</code></pre>
      <p>
         The heading is right there in both. Only the fixed dialog puts it in the dialog's name,
         which is the string a screen reader reads when the dialog opens.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>--role</code> keeps matching nodes and their ancestors, which is the fastest way to
         check a set of controls at once.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 tree http://localhost:4321/demo/team --role button</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>- main:
  - button "All"
  - button "Active"
  - button "Pending"
  - button "Invite member"
  - table "3 members in this workspace":
    - rowgroup:
      - row "Dana Whitfield dana@northwind.test Admin Active 2 hours ago":
        - cell:
          - button</code></pre>
      <p>
         The remove buttons have no name, matching the <code>button-name</code> violation. The
         three filter chips come back as buttons, which they are not: they are
         <code>&lt;div role="button"&gt;</code> with no <code>tabindex</code>. The tree cannot tell
         you that, because a role is all it reads. The keyboard can.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="keyboard">
      <h2>Tab order</h2>
      <p>
         <code>a1 sr</code> drives a screen reader. <code>--sr virtual</code> runs the simulated
         reader in headless Chromium, which needs no VoiceOver or NVDA setup and is what to use
         in a script. See <a href="/screen-readers">screen readers</a> for what the simulated
         reader does and does not model.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr start http://localhost:4321/demo/team --sr virtual
a1 sr press Tab --phrase</code></pre>
      <p>Eight presses of Tab on each page, one line per press:</p>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>/demo/team                                /demo/team/fixed

link, General                             link, General
link, Billing                             link, Billing
link, Team, current page                  link, Team, current page
searchbox, placeholder Filter members     searchbox, Filter members, placeholder Filter members
button, Invite member                     button, All, pressed
button                                    button, Active, not pressed
button                                    button, Pending, not pressed
button                                    button, Invite member</code></pre>
      <p>
         On the left, Tab goes from the filter field straight to Invite member. The three filter
         chips are not in the tab order at all, so a keyboard user cannot filter the table. The
         three unnamed <code>button</code> lines after it are the remove buttons.
      </p>
      <p>
         On the right the chips are <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> elements, so Tab reaches them, and
         <code>aria-pressed</code> makes the reader say <code>pressed</code> or
         <code>not pressed</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
         axe has a rule for this, <code>focus-order-semantics</code>, and it reports the page as
         inapplicable. The rule reads elements that are in the tab order and asks whether their
         role fits. An element left out of the tab order never reaches it.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="dialog">
      <h2>Opening the dialog</h2>
      <p>Move to the Invite member button, activate it, and read where the reader ended up.</p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr goto --name "Invite member" --role button
a1 sr activate
a1 sr read --phrase</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>/demo/team          button, Invite member
/demo/team/fixed    textbox, Email address, required</code></pre>
      <p>
         The broken page unhides a <code>div</code> and leaves focus on the button that opened it.
         A screen reader user hears nothing change. Tab from there walks the table behind the
         dialog instead of the fields inside it, and Escape does not close anything.
      </p>
      <p>
         The fixed page calls <code>showModal()</code> on a <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>. The
         browser moves focus to the first field, keeps Tab inside the dialog, makes the rest of
         the page inert, closes on Escape, and returns focus to the Invite member button
         afterwards. That is five behaviors for one method call, and no axe rule reports any of
         them.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>a1 wcag 2.4.3</code> and <code>a1 wcag 2.1.2</code> are the criteria this fails.
         <code>a1 audit</code> already listed both under <strong>Check by hand</strong>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="status">
      <h2>The confirmation message</h2>
      <p>
         Fill the form and submit it. <code>a1 sr checkpoint</code> marks the transcript so the
         check that follows reads only what came after it.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr goto --name "Email address"
a1 sr type "sam@northwind.test"
a1 sr checkpoint invite
a1 sr goto --name "Send invitation" --role button
a1 sr activate
a1 sr expect "Invitation sent" --since invite</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>/demo/team          Matched: nothing        Result: fail    exit 4
/demo/team/fixed    Matched: polite: Invitation sent to sam@northwind.test.    Result: pass    exit 0</code></pre>
      <p>
         Both pages show the same green confirmation on screen. The broken page builds a plain
         <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> and appends it, so a screen reader has no reason to read it and a
         user who never looks away from the form learns nothing. The fixed page renders
         <code>&lt;p class="banner" role="status"&gt;</code> from the start and sets its text, and
         the reader announces it without moving focus.
      </p>
      <p>
         The empty live region has to stay in the rendered page for this to work. The fixed page
         collapses it with <code>.banner:empty {'{'} padding: 0; border: 0; {'}'}</code> rather
         than <code>display: none</code>, which would take it out of the page and back to the
         broken behavior.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 wcag 4.1.3</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>4.1.3  Status Messages  [AA]

Testing it
  This needs a real page. Trigger a status message and confirm a screen reader announces it without moving
  focus.
    a1 sr expect &lt;text&gt;

If it fails
  ARIA22  Using role=status to present status messages  aria</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="passes">
      <h2>What axe called a pass</h2>
      <p>
         The filter field on the broken page is
         <code>&lt;input type="search" placeholder="Filter members"&gt;</code> and nothing else.
         axe's <code>label</code> rule accepts a non-empty placeholder as a name, so it passes,
         and <code>a1 audit --json</code> records criterion 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions with
         <code>axeVerdict: "pass"</code>.
      </p>
      <p>The reader disagrees:</p>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>/demo/team          searchbox, placeholder Filter members
/demo/team/fixed    searchbox, Filter members, placeholder Filter members</code></pre>
      <p>
         The placeholder is gone the moment the user types a character, and it is the only thing
         naming the field. The fix is a <code>&lt;label for="member-filter"&gt;</code>. Keep the
         placeholder or drop it, but do not let it be the label.
      </p>
      <p>
         Criterion 4.1.3 goes the other way. <code>a1 audit --json</code> records it as
         <code>applicability: "not-detected"</code> on the broken page, because a missing live
         region leaves nothing to detect. A criterion no tool claims is one to check by hand.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="mcp">
      <h2>The same run from an agent</h2>
      <p>
         Every MCP tool calls the same function as the matching command, so an agent gets the same
         results. Point your client at the server first.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="CONFIG"><code>{`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "a11ied": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "a11ied", "mcp"]
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="AI AGENT PROMPT"><code>Audit http://localhost:4321/demo/team with a11ied. Report every axe violation,
then check the three things axe cannot: whether Tab reaches the status filter
chips, whether opening the Invite member dialog moves focus into it, and whether
submitting the invite form announces a confirmation. Compare each answer against
http://localhost:4321/demo/team/fixed.</code></pre>
      <p>The tool calls that answer it:</p>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>audit</code>
            <span><code>target</code> the demo URL. Returns the three violations, the criterion
            rollup, and the heading levels.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>run_axe</code>
            <span><code>ruleIds: ["heading-order"]</code>, then
            <code>ruleIds: ["aria-dialog-name"]</code> against the <code>#invite</code> URL.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>tree</code>
            <span><code>role: "button"</code> for the control names, or the <code>#invite</code>
            URL for the dialog's name.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>sr_session</code>
            <span><code>action: "start"</code>, <code>target: "virtual"</code>,
            <code>allowVirtual: true</code>, <code>url</code> the demo page.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>sr_action</code>
            <span><code>action: "press"</code> with <code>keys: ["Tab"]</code> for the tab order,
            then <code>goto</code>, <code>activate</code>, <code>type</code>, and
            <code>checkpoint</code> for the dialog and the form.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>sr_expect</code>
            <span><code>pattern: "Invitation sent"</code>, <code>since: "invite"</code>. Returns
            <code>exitCode</code> 4 on the broken page and 0 on the fixed one.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>wcag_rule</code>
            <span><code>ruleId</code> for each failing rule, to get the criteria and the
            techniques that fix it.</span>
         </div>
      </div>
      <p>
         An agent that has the <a href="/guides/agent-skill">agent skill</a> installed already
         knows this order. See <a href="/guides/agents">test from an AI agent</a> for the full
         set of tools and <a href="/reference/mcp">the MCP reference</a> for their inputs.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="ledger" id="fixes">
      <h2>The ten fixes</h2>
      <p>
         Each row is one difference between the two pages, and each one is a few characters of
         markup or one method call.
      </p>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>image-alt</code>
            <span>Avatar images get <code>alt=""</code>. The name is already in the cell next to
            them, so an empty alt is the right answer, not a description.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>button-name</code>
            <span>Each remove button gets <code>aria-label="Remove Dana Whitfield"</code>, and its
            <code>svg</code> gets <code>aria-hidden="true"</code>.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>color-contrast</code>
            <span>The last-active dates move from <code>#a2aab5</code> to <code>#414b59</code>,
            8.84:1 on white. The Pending pill becomes <code>#6b4708</code> on
            <code>#fdf0d5</code>, 7.35:1.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>heading-order</code>
            <span>The Members heading becomes an <code>h2</code> under the page's
            <code>h1</code>.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>aria-dialog-name</code>
            <span>The dialog gets <code>aria-labelledby="invite-title"</code>, pointing at the
            heading it already had.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>2.1.1 Keyboard</code>
            <span>The filter chips become <code>&lt;button type="button"&gt;</code> instead of
            <code>&lt;div role="button"&gt;</code>, which puts them in the tab order and gives
            them Enter and Space for free.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>4.1.2 Name, Role, Value</code>
            <span>The chips get <code>aria-pressed</code>, so a screen reader says which one is
            selected. Without it, the dark background is the only thing that says so.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>2.4.3 Focus Order</code>
            <span>The dialog becomes a <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> opened with
            <code>showModal()</code>, which moves focus in, keeps it there, and returns it to the
            trigger on close.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>3.3.2 Labels or Instructions</code>
            <span>The filter field gets a visible
            <code>&lt;label for="member-filter"&gt;Filter members&lt;/label&gt;</code>.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>4.1.3 Status Messages</code>
            <span>The confirmation moves into a <code>role="status"</code> paragraph that is in
            the page before the form is submitted.</span>
         </div>
      </div>
      <p>
         Point the same commands at your own app next. <code>a1 audit</code> first, then
         <code>a1 tree</code> for the names and roles, then <code>a1 sr</code> for the five
         things neither of them can report.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
